WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.470
That is the Indian Point nuclear plant.

00:00:08.518 --> 00:00:12.472
It’s just 30 miles north of New York City
which is one reason 

00:00:12.472 --> 00:00:15.754
people have been fighting 
over this plant for decades.

00:00:15.754 --> 00:00:20.130
“More than 2,000 anti-nuclear protesters
gathered at the Indian Point power plant.”

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:24.070
“We’re in the danger zone and 
we better demonstrate today.”

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:28.434
"New York's Indian Point nuclear power plant
threatened with shut down 

00:00:28.434 --> 00:00:30.179
for lack of an approved 
evacuation plan."

00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:37.040
“If we ever had a major problem at Indian Point,
that might be a problem that we couldn't solve."

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.770
In early 2021, after years of protest, 
Indian Point finally shut down.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:46.716
“We’re celebrating the long-fought closure
of the Indian Point nuclear power plant."

00:00:46.716 --> 00:00:51.487
"Indian Point will close in 4 years, 
14 years ahead of schedule."

00:00:51.487 --> 00:00:55.784
"This is the power of the bully pulpit, 
the power of organizing."

00:00:55.784 --> 00:00:56.650
Here’s the problem.

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:00.759
Up until then, the vast majority of the electricity
used in New York City 

00:01:00.759 --> 00:01:05.878
that didn’t come from fossil fuels 
came from Indian Point.

00:01:05.878 --> 00:01:09.536
What happened here is an example of the 
complicated role nuclear energy is playing

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:11.769
in the fight against climate change.

00:01:15.803 --> 00:01:20.498
Nuclear plants generate about 10% of the electricity
that we use around the world.

00:01:20.498 --> 00:01:22.870
But 20% in the US.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:27.920
And 52% of the electricity in the US 
that’s not from fossil fuels.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.579
Experts widely agree that in order to slow
climate change, 

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:33.286
we need to use fewer carbon-emitting 
fossil fuels

00:01:33.286 --> 00:01:37.490
but the number of working nuclear
reactors in the US has been declining.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:42.058
ROBERTS: Each one that shuts down, it's like
a half a gig to a gigawatt of

00:01:42.058 --> 00:01:45.562
of mostly carbon free energy gone
from the grid.

00:01:45.562 --> 00:01:47.790
That’s longtime energy reporter David Roberts.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:53.100
ROBERTS: If you shut the nuclear plant down,
today, most likely, you're going to get a

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.670
bunch of natural gas to replace it.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:58.607
Take these three nuclear plants, shut down
in three different states.

00:01:58.607 --> 00:02:02.033
Here’s where these states got electricity
before the shutdowns.

00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:03.596
And after.

00:02:03.596 --> 00:02:06.326
More fossil fuels, in every case.

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:08.861
So far, that’s what happened 
at Indian Point too.

00:02:08.861 --> 00:02:12.142
In this case, nuclear was replaced 
with natural gas.

00:02:12.142 --> 00:02:17.230
ROBERTS: So then you have natural gas' local
pollution problems which are substantial.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.151
And then there's climate change.

00:02:19.151 --> 00:02:23.959
But, of course, when it comes to nuclear energy,
people have some concerns.

00:02:25.408 --> 00:02:26.291
This is Batu.

00:02:26.291 --> 00:02:28.219
Is Batu coming with us?

00:02:28.219 --> 00:02:29.297
He's my crew.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:34.509
I’ve come to see Indian Point for myself
with John Lipscomb and Richard Webster

00:02:34.509 --> 00:02:38.936
who are part of an environmental group that was
instrumental in the agreement to close the plant.

00:02:39.499 --> 00:02:43.569
LIPSCOMB: Twenty million people live within
50 miles of this plant.

00:02:43.569 --> 00:02:46.230
You can't afford an accident here.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.384
ROBERTS: People think about Chernobyl.

00:02:49.384 --> 00:02:52.507
ROBERTS: People think about Three Mile Island.

00:02:52.507 --> 00:02:57.169
ROBERTS: People think about the sort of famous
nuclear meltdowns.

00:02:57.169 --> 00:03:01.252
With Indian Point, people are worried about
a meltdown with a specific cause.

00:03:01.252 --> 00:03:05.488
“Al-Qaeda actually considered targeting
a nuclear power plant.”

00:03:05.488 --> 00:03:09.165
“It became clear to many of us that this
was a safety hazard.”

00:03:09.165 --> 00:03:11.480
“You put a little C4, you blow it up."

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.200
"See, you’re doing it Bob, you’re scaring
the hell outta me.”

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:18.188
People still argue about what would’ve actually
happened win the event of an attack

00:03:18.188 --> 00:03:23.579
but the fear of an attack was an important factor
turning public opinion against Indian Point.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:29.523
The thing is, the tragedies of nuclear disasters
create a skewed picture of how dangerous 

00:03:29.523 --> 00:03:31.730
nuclear energy actually is.

00:03:32.324 --> 00:03:35.329
All energy sources come with 
some degree of danger.

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:39.449
One common way to measure that danger is to
compare the number of deaths a type of energy

00:03:39.449 --> 00:03:44.439
has caused, like through accidents, or premature
deaths from pollution, with how much energy

00:03:44.439 --> 00:03:45.569
it provides.

00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:50.159
What we’d call “renewables” - solar,
wind, water power - these are extremely safe

00:03:50.159 --> 00:03:51.432
by this measure.

00:03:51.432 --> 00:03:54.037
Natural gas is less safe.

00:03:54.037 --> 00:03:58.090
Oil and coal are much, much more dangerous.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:00.199
Here’s nuclear.

00:04:00.199 --> 00:04:05.805
This takes into account all nuclear accidents,
including thousands of deaths from radiation.

00:04:05.805 --> 00:04:10.470
ROBERTS: So if you're choosing between nuclear
and fossil fuels purely on a safety basis,

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.716
there's no comparison.

00:04:12.716 --> 00:04:16.907
What’s so interesting is everyone I spoke
to agrees on these facts.

00:04:16.907 --> 00:04:19.079
They just interpret them differently.

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:22.196
To David, the numbers show how rare 
a nuclear disaster is.

00:04:22.196 --> 00:04:25.490
To John and Richard, the numbers 
are beside the point.

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:33.180
LIPSCOMB: That measure of normally occurring
deaths from various industries 

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.163
is a very valid study.

00:04:36.163 --> 00:04:40.590
But you can't have a nuclear meltdown 
in a solar farm.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:47.770
So there's the normal nominal casualties that
the industry develops.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:53.303
And then there's this nasty possibility 
that hangs out there.

00:04:53.303 --> 00:05:01.639
But human safety wasn’t the only concern
that led to the plant’s closing.

00:05:01.639 --> 00:05:05.889
When operating, Indian Point needed to take
in three Olympic swimming pools’ worth of

00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:09.060
Hudson River water to cool its reactors.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:10.990
The water went in through these grates.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:15.000
And then it was filtered through a grill,
then flowed on into the plant.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.748
It was supposed to keep fish and other organisms
from getting in and being killed

00:05:18.748 --> 00:05:20.389
but often, it didn’t.

00:05:20.389 --> 00:05:24.019
WEBSTER: And there are organisms that are either
pushed onto on the grill or 

00:05:24.019 --> 00:05:27.138
they're entrained in the water, go through the plant 
and get killed.

00:05:27.138 --> 00:05:30.478
And, you know, we estimate that about 
a billion organisms a year 

00:05:30.478 --> 00:05:33.870
were being killed in that way.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:36.669
LIPSCOMB: The ecosystem can't bear 
that kind of assault.

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:41.210
LIPSCOMB: We think the river has its own rights.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:46.370
In addition to the harm that the cooling system
did to wildlife, in 2015 a fire at the facility

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:49.727
leaked thousands of gallons of oil 
into the Hudson river.

00:05:49.727 --> 00:05:53.654
And in 2017 a deal was struck 
to shut down the plant.

00:05:53.654 --> 00:05:57.719
But many argue shutting down 
plants like these is short-sighted.

00:05:57.719 --> 00:06:01.473
ROBERTS: The people who have those concerns
are sincere and those concerns are real.

00:06:01.473 --> 00:06:10.410
But in all human affairs, you have to ask,
"compared to what"?

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:17.120
Climate change, if unrestrained, that is going
to be far more devastating for far more ecosystems,

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:23.539
and far more rivers and bodies of water than
any conceivable effect of, you know

00:06:23.539 --> 00:06:25.150
a nuclear power plant.

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:26.979
But Richard would say that’s a false choice.

00:06:26.979 --> 00:06:31.750
WEBSTER: I don't think it's a choice of 
emit carbon or kill fish.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.379
It's like those are not the choices we have.

00:06:33.379 --> 00:06:35.548
We have a third choice and we should use it.

00:06:35.548 --> 00:06:37.743
Long term, he might be right.

00:06:37.743 --> 00:06:39.690
But not in the short term.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.129
We just don’t have enough renewables yet.

00:06:42.129 --> 00:06:47.360
ROBERTS: Even if you replace that gigawatt
of lost power with renewables, that's a gigawatt

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.601
of renewables that isn't going to replace
fossil fuel plants, right?

00:06:52.601 --> 00:06:54.460
ROBERTS: It just moves your baseline back.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:58.050
ROBERTS: Right now, we just have lots and lots and
lots of fossil fuel power plants on the grid.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:02.690
So we don't have the time or luxury to sort
of optimize here.

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:06.278
Like, let's keep what we've got, is my take.

00:07:06.278 --> 00:07:10.947
And then there’s the third big factor at
play here, which is the reason these nuclear plants

00:07:10.947 --> 00:07:14.284
are so vulnerable to public opinion
in the first place.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:22.539
In the last 10 years, the price of electricity
from renewables and natural gas has plummeted

00:07:22.539 --> 00:07:25.749
as their production has gotten 
cheaper and easier.

00:07:25.749 --> 00:07:31.210
The price of electricity from nuclear has
gone up - in part because of regulation 

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.530
to address safety concerns.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:36.273
Existing nuclear plants are struggling 
to compete.

00:07:36.273 --> 00:07:40.270
And building new plants has become 
prohibitively expensive.

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:45.330
So when you combine cost plus the fear of
a disaster plus the environmental threats,

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:48.086
it's not hard to understand why 
nuclear plants are closing.

00:07:48.086 --> 00:07:51.894
But David says there's one final distinction
here that should be getting more attention.

00:07:51.894 --> 00:07:56.112
Something that nuclear energy has 
that renewables don't yet.

00:07:56.112 --> 00:07:59.127
ROBERTS: Wind and solar energy come and go
with the weather.

00:07:59.127 --> 00:08:03.599
So, you need power that's "firm."

00:08:03.599 --> 00:08:09.264
And "firm" just means you can turn it on when
you want to and run it as long as you want to.

00:08:09.264 --> 00:08:10.810
ROBERTS: We have tons of firm power now.

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:13.180
I mean most of the power on the grid 
right now is firm.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:16.924
ROBERTS: Every fossil fuel power plant counts
as "firm power."

00:08:16.924 --> 00:08:22.996
ROBERTS: But what you need if you're going
to decarbonize is clean firm power

00:08:22.996 --> 00:08:26.467
i.e. firm power that doesn't emit 
greenhouse gases.

00:08:26.467 --> 00:08:28.962
ROBERTS: That's much trickier.

00:08:28.962 --> 00:08:32.905
ROBERTS:There are not there are not as many
candidates for that.

00:08:32.905 --> 00:08:35.768
Nuclear is not the only option 
for clean firm power.

00:08:35.768 --> 00:08:38.652
But it is one we have right now.

00:08:38.652 --> 00:08:43.443
One that’s currently putting huge amounts
of power onto the electricity grid.

00:08:44.613 --> 00:08:48.216
What happened here at Indian Point shows that
fighting climate change is going to involve

00:08:48.216 --> 00:08:50.142
a lot of choices.

00:08:50.142 --> 00:08:53.280
On either side there are people that value
those choices differently.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.506
On the one hand, environmentalists 
and, on the other,

00:08:55.506 --> 00:08:58.270
as David likes to call them, “climate hawks”.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:03.650
ROBERTS: A “climate hawk” is someone who
places a high priority on the threat of climate

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:10.250
change and supports aggressive means 
of tackling it, trying to solve it.

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:13.680
In the long term, though, they want the same
thing: An energy future 

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.547
that harms the planet less.

